 = 0 \\).\n\n### Step 8: Find the Equation of \\( RY \\)\nSince \\( RY \\) is perpendicular to \\( PS \\), its slope is undefined, meaning it is a vertical line. Therefore, \\( RY \\) is the line \\( x = 6 \\).\n\n### Step 9: Find the Coordinates of \\( Q \\) and \\( R \\)\nFrom the coordinates:\n- \\( Q \\) is at \\((0, y)\\)\n- \\( R \\) is at \\((6, y)\\)\n\n### Step 10: Determine the Value of \\( y \\)\nSince \\( QX \\) is the line \\( x = 0 \\), and \\( QX \\) is perpendicular to \\( PS \\), the length of \\( QX \\) is the distance from \\( Q \\) to \\( X \\), which is \\( 2 \\). Therefore:\n\\[\nQX = |0 - 2| = 2\n\\]\nThis confirms that \\( QX = 2 \\).\n\n### Step 11: Find \\( QY \\)\nSince \\( Y \\) is at \\((6, 0)\\) and \\( Q \\) is at \\((0, y)\\), the distance \\( QY \\) is:\n\\[\nQY = \\sqrt{(6 - 0)^2 + (0 - y)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + y^2}\n\\]\nHowever, since \\( QX = 2 \\) and \\( QX \\) is perpendicular to \\( PS \\), and \\( PS = 6 \\), we can find \\( y \\) using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nQX^2 + PS^2 = QS^2 \\\\\n2^2 + 6^2 = y^2 \\\\\n4 + 36 = y^2 \\\\\ny^2 = 40 \\\\\ny = \\sqrt{40} = 2\\sqrt{10}\n\\]\nNow, substitute \\( y \\) back into the equation for \\( QY \\):\n\\[\nQY = \\sqrt{36 + (2\\sqrt{10})^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 40} = \\sqrt{76} = 2\\sqrt{19}\n\\]\n\n### Step 12: Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{19}}\n\\]",
